.TH AXCALL 1 "14 September 2026" "axcall" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axcall \- make an AX.25 connection over KISS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axcall
.RI [ options ]
.I port destination
.br
.B axcall
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-listen
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axcall
is a connected-mode AX.25 terminal. It dials a remote station, pipes your
keyboard to it and its replies to your screen, and disconnects cleanly. As is
normal in AX.25 it runs entirely in line mode, and CR/LF translation is done
transparently in both directions. It is 8 bit clean and always UTF\-8.
.PP
It is not the
.B axcall
that came with ax25\-apps. That one opened an
.B AF_AX25
socket on the Linux kernel AX.25 stack, which has since been withdrawn. This one
carries its own AX.25 v2.2 data link and speaks KISS to a TNC over a serial port
or over TCP, so it needs no kernel support,
.BR kissattach (8)
or
.BR axparms (8),
and no privilege. The command line is deliberately the old one, so existing
scripts and existing habits mostly carry over. See
.B COMPATIBILITY
for where they do not.
.PP
The program provides no terminal emulation, no file transfer and no
.RB ` ~ '
escapes. It is a pipe with a link layer under it.
.SH PORT
The
.I port
argument says which TNC to talk to. It is one of three things, told apart by
shape before anything is opened:
.TP
.I name
A name from the ports file, for example
.BR radio .
See
.BR "PORTS FILE" .
.TP
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 " or " /dev/ttyUSB0:57600
A serial device, optionally with the baud rate. Anything starting with
.RB ` / ',
.RB ` ./ '
or
.RB ` ../ '
is taken as a path.
.TP
.I 10.45.0.66:8001
A KISS\-over\-TCP endpoint. A bracketed IPv6 literal such as
.I [::1]:8001
works too.
.PP
.B \-\-serial
and
.B \-\-tcp
give the same two transports explicitly. When either is used the single
positional argument is the
.IR destination .
.SH OPTIONS
These are spelled as the kernel AX.25
.B axcall
spelled them.
.TP
.BI \-s " mycall"
Connect using the specified source callsign. Required unless the port names one.
.TP
.BI \-p " paclen"
The largest amount of data to send in each frame, N1 (PACLEN). 1 to 1024,
default 256. XID may lower it to the peer's. The kernel version stopped at 500.
.TP
.BI \-w " window"
The send window, k (MAXFRAME): frames in flight before an acknowledgement is
needed. 1 to 7, or 1 to 127 with
.BR "\-m e" .
The default is 4 on either modulus, after which XID takes the lower of what each
end offers. k is a notification of what a station can receive, not a request, so
a wide window has to be set at
.I both
ends: asking for 100 against a station offering 7 settles on 7. With SREJ
negotiated the window is held to half the modulus: on modulo 8 that is 4, so 5 to
7 take effect only on a go\-back\-N link, and on modulo 128 it is 64. A v2.2
link negotiates SREJ with any peer that offers it, so a modulo\-128 window above
64 usually runs at 64; the status line says so, as
.BR "window 100 (64 in effect)" .
.TP
.BI \-m " s|e"
The AX.25 mode to use:
.B s
for normal (modulo 8) operation, the default, and
.B e
for extended (modulo 128, AX.25 v2.2). An extended dial falls back to modulo 8
if the peer answers FRMR or DM, so the call still goes through. Inbound sessions
use whichever the caller asks for.
.IP
An extended dial sends its SABME first and negotiates afterwards, so the
parameters on the connect line are the ones this end offered. When the XID
exchange settles on something else a round trip later, axcall says so:
.IP
.EX
axcall: connected to GB7RDG (mod\-128, window 32, paclen 128, SREJ on)
axcall: negotiated with GB7RDG (mod\-128, window 7, paclen 128, SREJ on)
.EE
.IP
A modulo\-8 dial negotiates before it connects, so its connect line is already
the agreed one and no second line follows.
.TP
.BI \-T " timeout"
Close the connection automatically when no data has been transferred in either
direction for this many seconds. Decimals are allowed, so a value of 0.5 is
500 ms. axcall exits 5 when it fires.
.IP
This is the opposite of
.BR \-\-keepalive ,
which is T3 and polls to hold an idle link
.IR up .
Only data resets it: a keepalive poll does not count, matching the kernel
version, where an RR never woke the socket. Unlike the kernel version, axcall
hangs up properly and waits for the handshake rather than dropping the socket
and leaving the peer to time out.
.TP
.B \-W
Wait for the remote to disconnect even when standard input has closed. Normally
.B "echo q | axcall radio gb7rdg"
closes the moment
.B echo
finishes, so the reply from gb7rdg is never seen. With
.B \-W
axcall stops reading input but leaves the link up and keeps printing until the
peer hangs up. Pair it with
.BR \-T ,
or a peer that never hangs up will leave axcall waiting for ever.
.TP
.B \-S
Be silent: suppress the status lines. Useful in shell scripts. Errors are still
reported, because this silences progress and not diagnostics. It matters less
here than it did in the kernel version, which printed its banner and
.RB \(lq "*** Connected to" \(rq
to standard output; axcall keeps standard output to received data alone.
.TP
.B \-d
Write one line per frame, in both directions, to standard error. The kernel
version's
.B \-d
set
.B SO_DEBUG
on the socket; this reports the frames axcall itself sent and received:
.IP
.in +4n
.EX
18:45:51.123 > M0LTE\-7>GB7RDG SABM C P
18:45:51.520 < GB7RDG>M0LTE\-7 UA R F
18:45:51.521 > M0LTE\-7>GB7RDG I C ns=0 nr=0 pid=F0 len=12
.EE
.in
.IP
The direction, the addresses, the frame type, then
.B C
or
.B R
for command or response and
.B P
or
.B F
for the poll/final bit, then whatever that frame type carries. Independent of
.BR \-S ,
so
.B \-d \-S
gives a bare trace with no status around it.
.TP
.BI \-b " l|e"
The method of backoff. Accepted and ignored: this axcall adapts T1 from the
measured round trip and has no linear/exponential selector. The argument is
still checked, so a stray
.B \-b 9600
is reported rather than passing quietly.
.TP
.B \-r
Raw mode: a byte pipe. Standard input reaches the link exactly as it arrives
and the link reaches standard output exactly as it arrives, with no line
framing, no CR appended on send and no CR\-to\-LF translation on receive. Input
is read in frame\-sized bites, as the kernel version read
.I paclen
at a time.
.IP
With
.B \-S
this is a clean 8 bit pipe and nothing else, which is what the kernel version
meant by "-r together with -S in order to be really transparent". It is what
makes axcall usable in a shell pipeline, or as an
.BR ssh (1)
ProxyCommand.
.TP
.B \-t
Line mode: read a line, send it CR\-terminated, and translate received CR and
CRLF to LF so the terminal advances a line. The default, and the opposite of
.BR \-r ;
if both are given the last one wins.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-8
No remote commands, and UTF\-8. Both are accepted and ignored: axcall is always
UTF\-8 in line mode, and has no remote commands (#BIN#, 7plus, //echo) to
disable.
.TP
.B \-v
Display the version, including the versions of the SDL spec tables and the
runtime libraries it was built against.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a usage summary. Note that the kernel version's
.B \-h
selects slave mode; see
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
The options below have no counterpart in the kernel version, and are spelled
long so they cannot collide with the letters above.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device[:baud]"
Use this serial device, instead of naming it as the
.I port
argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-tcp " host:port"
Use KISS over TCP, instead of naming it as the
.I port
argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate, default 57600. Overrides a
.RB ` :baud '
suffix. Ignored for a TCP port.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-listen
Wait for an inbound connection instead of calling out. The kernel stack left
this to
.BR ax25d (8).
.TP
.B \-\-mod128
The same as
.BR "\-m e" .
.TP
.BI \-\-keepalive " seconds"
The idle\-link poll interval, T3, default 300. With no traffic for this long
axcall sends an RR poll to check the link is still up. Must be a positive whole
number: the link layer has no "never poll" setting, so a zero timer would poll
continuously. This
.I keeps a link up
and is not the kernel version's
.BR \-T ,
which drops one.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " n"
Retries before the link is dropped, N2 (RETRY). 1 to 255, default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-frack " seconds"
The initial acknowledgement timeout, T1 (FRACK). 0.5 to 60, decimals allowed,
default 6. Only the starting point: T1 is adapted from the measured round trip
after the first exchanges.
.TP
.BI \-\-ack\-delay " seconds"
The acknowledgement delay, T2 (RESPTIME). 0 to 30, decimals allowed, default 3.
Received frames are acknowledged together once this has elapsed; 0 acknowledges
every frame at once.
.TP
.BI \-\-txdelay " ms"
KISS TXDELAY: how long the TNC holds the carrier before it starts sending data.
Milliseconds, in steps of 10, up to 2550.
.TP
.BI \-\-persist " n"
KISS persistence, 0 to 255. With
.B \-\-slottime
this is the p-persistent CSMA the TNC uses to decide when it may key up on a
busy channel.
.TP
.BI \-\-slottime " ms"
KISS slot time. Milliseconds, in steps of 10, up to 2550.
.TP
.BI \-\-txtail " ms"
KISS TXTAIL. Milliseconds, in steps of 10. Deprecated on modern TNCs, and
present because old firmware wants it.
.IP
These four are the KISS channel-access parameters, and they are the one part of
this program that the kernel AX.25
.B axcall
had no equivalent for, because it did not need one. They describe the radio
channel rather than any one connection, so under the kernel stack they were set
per interface by
.BR kissparms (8),
once, for every program using the port. There is no
.BR kissattach (8)
and no shared interface now: axcall holds the only handle on the TNC, so if it
does not set them, nothing does.
.IP
Milliseconds are used here because that is what
.BR kissparms (8)
took. The wire unit is 10 ms, so a value that is not a multiple of ten is
refused rather than rounded. Nothing is sent unless asked for, so a TNC that has
been set up deliberately is left alone.
.IP
The ports file is usually the better home for these: that a radio needs 300 ms
of TX delay is a fact about the port, not about a call made over it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-xid
Skip the XID exchange sent before the SABM on a modulo\-8 dial. Without it axcall
offers SREJ (selective retransmit) and its window; with it the link is plain
go\-back\-N. Outbound only; an accepted
.B \-m e
dial negotiates XID after the UA regardless.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The same as
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-help
The same as
.BR \-h .
.PP
.BR \-T ", " \-W ", " \-S ", " \-d ", " \-r " and " \-t
also have the long spellings
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ", " \-\-wait ", " \-\-silent ", " \-\-debug ", "
.BR \-\-raw " and " \-\-talk .
.PP
On end of input axcall finishes sending whatever is still queued or
unacknowledged before it hangs up, so
.B "cat file | axcall \-r radio gb7rdg"
delivers the whole file rather than the first window of it. Interrupting with
Ctrl\-C does not wait: it hangs up at once.
.SH PORTS FILE
The ports file binds a short name to a transport, a callsign and optional link
defaults, so that
.B axcall radio gb7rdg
works with nothing else on the line. It is the replacement for
.BR axports (5),
which named a callsign, a paclen and a window but no device, because
.BR kissattach (8)
bound the tty to the port name separately.
.PP
Columns are whitespace\-separated. The first three are mandatory. A
.RB ` \- '
means "not set" in any of the optional ones, and anything after the window
column is a description that is read by people and ignored by axcall. Blank
lines and lines beginning with
.RB ` # '
are skipped.
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
# name   callsign   transport            paclen  window  description
radio    M0LTE\-7    /dev/ttyUSB0:57600   256     4       144.800 MHz
node     M0LTE\-7    10.45.0.66:8001      \-       \-       LinBPQ
.EE
.in
.PP
After the window column, any number of
.IR key = value
settings may appear before the description. The recognised keys are
.BR txdelay ", " persist ", " slottime " and " txtail ,
in the same units as the options above:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
radio  M0LTE\-7  /dev/ttyUSB0:57600  256  4  txdelay=300 persist=63  144.800 MHz
.EE
.in
.PP
A token is only read as a setting if its key is one of those four, so a
description is free to contain an equals sign.
.PP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
is read first and
.I ~/.config/axcall/ports
second, the second replacing same\-named entries from the first. A malformed line
fails the whole load rather than being skipped, so a typo is reported instead of
quietly becoming "unknown port".
.PP
Precedence throughout is that a command\-line flag beats the ports file, which
beats the built\-in default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ran and the link was closed.
.TP
.B 1
A fatal error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: the command line, or the ports file.
.TP
.B 3
The modem could not be opened: no such serial device, permission denied, or the
TCP connection was refused.
.TP
.B 4
The connect was refused by the peer (DM) or timed out.
.TP
.B 5
The
.B \-T
idle timeout fired and closed the link.
.PP
Note that the kernel version exits 0 even when the connect fails, so a script
that silently tolerated a dead link will start reporting one.
.SH EXAMPLES
A terminal session, which is the default:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
axcall radio gb7rdg
.EE
.in
.PP
Send a file. The receiving end has no input of its own, so it needs
.B \-W
to stop it hanging up the moment its standard input ends:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
# receiving station
axcall \-l \-r \-S \-W \-s M0LTE\-1 radio > received.bin

# sending station
cat file.bin | axcall \-r \-S radio m0lte\-1
.EE
.in
.PP
axcall finishes sending what is queued before it hangs up, so the whole file
arrives and not just the first window.
.PP
Send something and wait for the reply, which is the pairing worth remembering:
.B \-W
holds the link open past the end of input, and
.B \-T
closes it once the far end has gone quiet. This exits 5.
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
echo "help" | axcall \-W \-T 10 radio gb7rdg
.EE
.in
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
When set, names the single ports file to read, instead of the two default paths.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
System ports file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/axcall/ports
Per\-user ports file, overriding same\-named entries in the system one.
.RB ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is honoured.)
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The command line is the kernel AX.25
.BR axcall 's,
with these differences.
.PP
.B \-h
means
.BR \-\-help ,
not slave mode. Slave mode was one of three curses screen modes that do not
exist here, and
.B \-h
for help is near\-universal. On its own it prints the usage summary; alongside
other arguments it is a usage error, because printing help and exiting 0 would
look to a ported script like a successful call.
.PP
.B \-i
(IBM850) is refused. Ignoring it would produce mojibake rather than nothing.
.PP
A digipeater path after the
.IR destination ,
with or without the literal
.BR via ,
is refused rather than ignored. axcall dials direct only.
.PP
NET/ROM and Rose are not supported. The kernel version picked the address family
by trying the port name against
.BR axports (5),
then
.BR nrports (5),
then
.BR rsports (5),
and the same binary was installed as
.B netromcall
and
.BR rosecall .
.PP
There is no ASCII, YAPP, YAPP\-C or 7plus file transfer, no
.RB ` ~ '
escape commands, no status line and no menu bar.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR axlisten (1),
.BR ax25 (4)
.PP
